# Grouper line-ending and diff policy
#
# Verified before adding: all 125 tracked blobs are already LF-only in storage, so the
# rules below change nothing that is committed. They make the existing behaviour explicit
# instead of leaving it to each clone's local core.autocrlf setting -- a clone with
# autocrlf=false, or on a non-Windows host, would otherwise check out LF and drift from
# the rest of the tree, which is uniformly CRLF.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Text: stored LF in the repository, checked out CRLF in the working tree.
# Covers .cs, .csproj, .sln, .resx, .config, .json, .sql, .ps1, .md, LICENSE.
# Visual Studio needs CRLF in .sln files, which this guarantees on every host.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary: never normalize, never diff as text. ("binary" == "-text -diff".)
#
# No binary file is tracked today -- .gitignore covers bin/, obj/, .vs/, *.pdb and
# the certificate types. These rules are insurance for a future `git add -f`, so a
# stray artefact cannot be corrupted by line-ending conversion on the way in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
*.pfx binary
*.cer binary

# Deliberately NOT listed: .vs/** and per-user IDE state. .gitignore already keeps
# them out, and "text=auto" detects genuinely binary content by its NUL bytes without
# help. Forcing a path binary is not free -- it makes git demand a byte-for-byte match
# against the working tree, which turns any already-normalized text file underneath
# that path into a phantom modification.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Language-aware diff hunk headers (git built-in drivers).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.cs text diff=csharp
*.md text diff=markdown
